DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aallqq facility provides an asynchronous fixed or variable length
     recording mechanism, known as Asynchronous Logging Queues.  It can record
     to any vnode(9), thus providing the ability to journal logs to character
     devices as well as regular files.  All functions accept a _s_t_r_u_c_t _a_l_q ar‐
     gument, which is an opaque type that maintains state information for an
     Asynchronous Logging Queue.  The logging facility runs in a separate ker‐
     nel thread, which services all log entry requests.

     An “asynchronous log entry” is defined as _s_t_r_u_c_t _a_l_e, which has the fol‐
     lowing members:

           struct ale {
                   intptr_t        ae_bytesused;   /* # bytes written to ALE. */
                   char            *ae_data;       /* Write ptr. */
                   int             ae_pad;         /* Unused, compat. */
           };

     An aallqq can be created in either fixed or variable length mode.  A vari‐
     able length aallqq accommodates writes of varying length using aallqq__wwrriitteenn()
     and aallqq__ggeettnn().  A fixed length aallqq accommodates a fixed number of writes
     using aallqq__wwrriittee() and aallqq__ggeett(), each of fixed size (set at queue cre‐
     ation time).  Fixed length mode is deprecated in favour of variable
     length mode.

FFUUNNCCTTIIOONNSS
     The aallqq__ooppeenn__ffllaaggss() function creates a new variable length asynchronous
     logging queue.  The _f_i_l_e argument is the name of the file to open for
     logging.  If the file does not yet exist, aallqq__ooppeenn() will attempt to cre‐
     ate it.  The _c_m_o_d_e argument will be passed to vvnn__ooppeenn() as the requested
     creation mode, to be used if the file will be created by aallqq__ooppeenn().
     Consumers of this API may wish to pass ALQ_DEFAULT_CMODE, a default cre‐
     ation mode suitable for most applications.  The _c_r_e_d argument specifies
     the credentials to use when opening and performing I/O on the file.  The
     _s_i_z_e argument sets the size (in bytes) of the underlying queue.  The
     ALQ_ORDERED flag may be passed in via _f_l_a_g_s to indicate that the ordering
     of writer threads waiting for a busy aallqq to free up resources should be
     preserved.

     The deprecated aallqq__ooppeenn() function is implemented as a wrapper around
     aallqq__ooppeenn__ffllaaggss() to provide backwards compatibility to consumers that
     have not been updated to utilise the newer aallqq__ooppeenn__ffllaaggss() function.  It
     passes all arguments through to aallqq__ooppeenn__ffllaaggss() untouched except for
     _s_i_z_e and _c_o_u_n_t, and sets _f_l_a_g_s to 0.  To create a variable length mode
     aallqq, the _s_i_z_e argument should be set to the size (in bytes) of the under‐
     lying queue and the _c_o_u_n_t argument should be set to 0.  To create a fixed
     length mode aallqq, the _s_i_z_e argument should be set to the size (in bytes)
     of each write and the _c_o_u_n_t argument should be set to the number of _s_i_z_e
     byte chunks to reserve capacity for.

     The aallqq__wwrriitteenn() function writes _l_e_n bytes from _d_a_t_a to the designated
     variable length mode queue _a_l_q.  If aallqq__wwrriitteenn() could not write the en‐
     try immediately and ALQ_WAITOK is set in _f_l_a_g_s, the function will be al‐
     lowed to msleep_spin(9) with the “alqwnord” or “alqwnres” wait message.
     A write will automatically schedule the queue _a_l_q to be flushed to disk.
     This behaviour can be controlled by passing ALQ_NOACTIVATE via _f_l_a_g_s to
     indicate that the write should not schedule _a_l_q to be flushed to disk.

     The deprecated aallqq__wwrriittee() function is implemented as a wrapper around
     aallqq__wwrriitteenn() to provide backwards compatibility to consumers that have
     not been updated to utilise variable length mode queues.  The function
     will write _s_i_z_e bytes of data (where _s_i_z_e was specified at queue creation
     time) from the _d_a_t_a buffer to the _a_l_q.  Note that it is an error to call
     aallqq__wwrriittee() on a variable length mode queue.

     The aallqq__fflluusshh() function is used for flushing _a_l_q to the log medium that
     was passed to aallqq__ooppeenn().  If _a_l_q has data to flush and is not already in
     the process of being flushed, the function will block doing IO.  Other‐
     wise, the function will return immediately.

     The aallqq__cclloossee() function will close the asynchronous logging queue _a_l_q
     and flush all pending write requests to the log medium.  It will free all
     resources that were previously allocated.

     The aallqq__ggeettnn() function returns an asynchronous log entry from _a_l_q, ini‐
     tialised to point at a buffer capable of receiving _l_e_n bytes of data.
     This function leaves _a_l_q in a locked state, until a subsequent aallqq__ppoosstt()
     or aallqq__ppoosstt__ffllaaggss() call is made.  If aallqq__ggeettnn() could not obtain _l_e_n
     bytes of buffer immediately and ALQ_WAITOK is set in _f_l_a_g_s, the function
     will be allowed to msleep_spin(9) with the “alqgnord” or “alqgnres” wait
     message.  The caller can choose to write less than _l_e_n bytes of data to
     the returned asynchronous log entry by setting the entry's ae_bytesused
     field to the number of bytes actually written.  This must be done prior
     to calling aallqq__ppoosstt().

     The deprecated aallqq__ggeett() function is implemented as a wrapper around
     aallqq__ggeettnn() to provide backwards compatibility to consumers that have not
     been updated to utilise variable length mode queues.  The asynchronous
     log entry returned will be initialised to point at a buffer capable of
     receiving _s_i_z_e bytes of data (where _s_i_z_e was specified at queue creation
     time).  Note that it is an error to call aallqq__ggeett() on a variable length
     mode queue.

     The aallqq__ppoosstt__ffllaaggss() function schedules the asynchronous log entry _a_l_e
     (obtained from aallqq__ggeettnn() or aallqq__ggeett()) for writing to _a_l_q.  The
     ALQ_NOACTIVATE flag may be passed in via _f_l_a_g_s to indicate that the queue
     should not be immediately scheduled to be flushed to disk.  This function
     leaves _a_l_q in an unlocked state.

     The aallqq__ppoosstt() function is implemented as a wrapper around
     aallqq__ppoosstt__ffllaaggss() to provide backwards compatibility to consumers that
     have not been updated to utilise the newer aallqq__ppoosstt__ffllaaggss() function.  It
     simply passes all arguments through to aallqq__ppoosstt__ffllaaggss() untouched, and
     sets _f_l_a_g_s to 0.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     The aallqq__wwrriitteenn() and aallqq__wwrriittee() functions both perform a bcopy(3) from
     the supplied _d_a_t_a buffer into the underlying aallqq buffer.  Performance
     critical code paths may wish to consider using aallqq__ggeettnn() (variable
     length queues) or aallqq__ggeett() (fixed length queues) to avoid the extra mem‐
     ory copy.  Note that a queue remains locked between calls to aallqq__ggeettnn()
     or aallqq__ggeett() and aallqq__ppoosstt() or aallqq__ppoosstt__ffllaaggss(), so this method of writ‐
     ing to a queue is unsuitable for situations where the time between calls
     may be substantial.

LLOOCCKKIINNGG
     Each asynchronous logging queue is protected by a spin mutex.

     Functions aallqq__fflluusshh() and aallqq__ooppeenn() may attempt to acquire an internal
     sleep mutex, and should consequently not be used in contexts where sleep‐
     ing is not allowed.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The aallqq__ooppeenn() function returns one of the error codes listed in open(2),
     if it fails to open _f_i_l_e, or else it returns 0.

     The aallqq__wwrriitteenn() and aallqq__wwrriittee() functions return EWOULDBLOCK if
     ALQ_NOWAIT was set in _f_l_a_g_s and either the queue is full or the system is
     shutting down.

     The aallqq__ggeettnn() and aallqq__ggeett() functions return NULL if ALQ_NOWAIT was set
     in _f_l_a_g_s and either the queue is full or the system is shutting down.

     NOTE: invalid arguments to non-void functions will result in undefined
     behaviour.

